An extension to bocl: init, abort and quit hooks


These are specification elements to be added to the specifications,
and to implement and document in the user-manual and implementation
documentation (chapter on extensions).

| variable          | default value | description                                 |
|-------------------+---------------+---------------------------------------------|
|                   |               |                                             |
| BOCL:*INIT-HOOK*  | ()            | A list of function designators.             |
|                   |               | signature: (lambda () …)                    |
|                   |               | Each function is called inside an           |
|                   |               | IGNORE-ERRORS in turn, after the            |
|                   |               | rc (init) file has been loaded (or          |
|                   |               | after processing the --no-init option).     |
|                   |               |                                             |
| BOCL:*ABORT-HOOK* | ()            | A list of function designators.             |
|                   |               | signature: (lambda (signum) …)              |
|                   |               | Each function is called inside an           |
|                   |               | IGNORE-ERRORS in turn, when it has been     |
|                   |               | determined the process is crashing          |
|                   |               | (exiting upon reception of a signal).       |
|                   |               | The signal may indicate a corruption        |
|                   |               | of the system, so the garbage collector     |
|                   |               | is disabled, and minimal dynamic allocation |
|                   |               | should be performed. It's a best effort,    |
|                   |               | no guarantees.                              |
|                   |               |                                             |
| BOCL:*QUIT-HOOK*  | ()            | A list of function designators.             |
|                   |               | signature: (lambda (status) …)              |
|                   |               | Each function is called inside an           |
|                   |               | IGNORE-ERRORS in turn, before BOCL:QUIT     |
|                   |               | terminates the process. It's a normal       |
|                   |               | exit, so all facilities are available,      |
|                   |               | but functions cannot prevent exiting once   |
|                   |               | they return.                                |
|                   |               |                                             |

When bocl boots (after it has loaded all system files like its
prelude), and once it has processed its init (rc) file (or ignored
it), each function on BOCL:*INIT-HOOK* are called in turn in a
IGNORE-ERRORS form.  Note: if a crash occurs, or one of those 
functions call BOCL:QUIT, then those processes happen, with the
corresponding hooks processed, and the init function doesn't return
and none the remaining functions on the BOCL:*INIT-HOOK* are called.

When bocl quits (BOCL:QUIT [status]) is called, bocl performs a
non-local exit that allows UNWIND-PROTECT forms to perform clean-ups
(closing files, network connections, etc).  Once this done, the
functions on the BOCL:*QUIT-HOOK* are called in turn, each inside an
IGNORE-ERRORS form.  Note: if a crash occurs, then crash processes
happen, with the corresponding hooks processed, and the quit hook
function doesn't return and none the remaining functions on the
BOCL:*QUIT-HOOK* are called.


When a crash occurs (eg. a signal is received that means termination
of the POSIX process), bocl processes the crash, stops the
garbage-collector, and as a last ressort to save something, call in
turn each functions on this BOCL:*ABORT-HOOK*, in an IGNORE-ERRORS
forms, passing it the signum.

In both BOCL:*QUIT-HOOK* functions and BOCL:*ABORT-HOOK* functions,
length processes are discouraged. Eg. if a quit hook function lasts to
long, the user may become impatient and kill the process with a SIGINT
or SIGTERM, so the quit hook function won't be able to terminate, the
remaining quit hook functions won't be called, and the abort hook
functions will have a chance, to in a very limited environment.

They may be used to flush buffers, and save a dirty/clean flag on
disk, at most.  The actual data should have been written or processed
before.

These hook functions may be called from a different thread than the
main thread. But the main thread will start processing the main task
(-l -x -i options, with the REPL), only once the hook functions are
completed.

Note: these lists are not cleared, even while they serve only once
(contrarily to equivalent lists in a saved lisp image, which can be
reused. These lists must be populated by user code, from the init (rc)
file, the command-line options or normal lisp code.

Note: no mutex or other protection to these variables is
provided. Normally, SETQ should not crash, so setting the abort hook
should be safe, unless several threads try to do the same at the same
time.


Function BOCL:QUIT

We precise here that this function shall perform a non-local exit, so
that any unwind-protect with clean-up clauses pending in the current
dynamic environment have a chance to perform the clean-up. Being the
primitive form used by WITH-OPEN-FILE and all similar macros managing
resources, this allows them to clean-up and close and free resources,
including external resources (flush buffer, close files, close
sockets, free external resources).

The BOCL:*QUIT-HOOK* can be used for clean-up that doesn't lead itself
to dynamic scope unwind-protecting. For example a file open with OPEN,
and intended to remain open for the whole process, may be flushed and
closed properly in a BOCL:*QUIT-HOOK*. Similarly, if the terminal is
initialized with ncurses, a BOCL:*QUIT-HOOK* and BOCL:*ABORT-HOOK* can
be used to reset its state.


;; ============================== RESOLUTION (Slice 58) ==============================
;; Implemented:
;;  - BOCL package (created in C, uses CL).
;;  - BOCL:*INIT-HOOK* / *ABORT-HOOK* / *QUIT-HOOK* (prelude defvars, exported).
;;  - BOCL:QUIT [status]: non-local exit via THROW to a top-level CATCH that
;;    main() establishes (bocl_run_with_toplevel_catch in eval.c), so pending
;;    UNWIND-PROTECT clean-ups run; then *QUIT-HOOK* runs; then exit(status).
;;  - *INIT-HOOK* fired by main() after the init/rc files, before the main task
;;    (the whole session runs inside the QUIT catch).
;;  - *ABORT-HOOK* fired by the crash handler (kernel.c), GC disabled, only when
;;    a hook is registered (no perturbation of ordinary crashes).
;;  - Documented in documentation/documentation.org (Extensions > The REPL).
;; Verified: QUIT runs UNWIND-PROTECT cleanup + quit-hook and exits with STATUS;
;; INIT-HOOK fires before the main task; no regression in normal runs.
